    <section id="cs-top">
        <h1>Launchpad</h1>
        <h2>A self-serve framework for deploying prototype tools and agents, with governance and a real path to production built in, plus the four-hour habit that fed it.</h2>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-problem">
        <h3>The situation</h3>
        <p>By late 2025, Mod Op had hundreds of people experimenting with generative AI. Good ideas were everywhere: in decks, docs, side projects, and demos that impressed a room and then evaporated. The agency's AI investment risked becoming a collection of disconnected experiments: lots of energy, no portfolio, no way to compare an agent idea against a tool idea against a client-offering idea.</p>
        <p>The real problem wasn't generating ideas. It was that the cost of evaluating them was too high. A business case takes weeks. A deck persuades without proving anything. And a full build is too expensive to throw away when the signal comes back weak.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-role">
        <h3>What I owned</h3>
        <p>I originated the prototyping motion and ran it end-to-end across the Q1 2026 hackathons: prioritization of what got built, demo structure and pacing, office hours, daily scrums, and the presentations of outputs to Growth and AI Council audiences. I drove the connection between prototypes and go-to-market decisions: which concepts became offerings, which became client conversations, which got killed. I designed the platform concept behind it and worked with engineering on the architecture.</p>
        <p>This was cross-functional by design. Engineering, product management, UX, data science, and strategy all built and judged. My job was to make the system produce evidence, not theater.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-insight">
        <h3>The insight</h3>
        <p>The cheapest way to evaluate an AI idea is not a document. It's a rough working version plus a one-minute demo, shown to real colleagues and real clients. Something half-built and real gets you a reaction you can trust. A polished hypothetical only gets you an opinion.</p>
        <p><strong>Compress the cost of finding out whether an idea is good. Build enough of the real thing to create an informed reaction, then invest only when the signal justifies it.</strong></p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-system">
        <h3>How it worked</h3>
        <p><strong>The operating model:</strong> prototype in about four hours &rarr; scope document in four more &rarr; engineering design &rarr; MVP &rarr; production. Nothing moved forward until a prototype produced strong user or client signal, or an explicit commitment. We triaged roughly 100 backlog ideas through this filter. Nothing got funded just because it sounded good in a meeting.</p>
        <p><strong>The platform:</strong> a Next.js 15 monorepo hosting 66+ isolated prototype applications at launch, each one a deployable app rather than a mockup. Nexus OAuth 2.0 authentication so anything worth testing ran against our real single sign-on, inheriting the platform's permissions and governance from the start. PostgreSQL engagement tracking underneath, so interest was measured and not just asserted. Every prototype shipped with a one-minute demo video, dual metadata configuration, reactions, commentary, and client mentions. A bounty system let anyone in the agency propose ideas and see them picked up.</p>
        <p>The prototypes were real UIs with basic AI and data behavior (retrieval, agents, image generation), because the point was to provoke informed reactions. The Q1 2026 hackathons produced 60+ working prototypes across clusters spanning infrastructure, subscriptions, delivery, services, and white-label/enterprise products.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-adoption">
        <h3>Adoption</h3>
        <p>Launchpad became how the agency evaluated AI opportunities. Growth teams used its engagement metrics to decide what to take to clients. The roadmap process shifted from opinion-driven to signal-driven: roughly 100 backlog items were triaged into a ranked portfolio with explicit advancement criteria. GEO/AEO (generative-engine optimization) and Digital Twins emerged from the pipeline as the first priority MVPs.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-commercial">
        <h3>Commercial consequence</h3>
        <p>By August 2026, more than 70 client accounts were in active conversations about Launchpad-derived concepts: GEO, digital twins, competitive intelligence, content and research tools. Pipeline, not booked revenue.</p>
        <p>The sharpest proof came from Sparkling Ice and Talking Rain: prototypes built on the client's own brand data, demoed live in the room with product and engineering at the table. Working software beat the capability deck. The session converted into an AI-readiness scoping engagement and became the playbook for how the agency runs major client AI meetings.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-changed">
        <h3>What changed</h3>
        <p>The organization stopped asking "should we explore this?" and started asking "which prototype earned the next hour?" Investment decisions got faster and more honest. Innovation became a measurable system with a backlog, a filter, and real outcomes, instead of a slide in a capabilities deck.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-learned">
        <h3>What I learned</h3>
        <p>Demos create shared truth in a way documents never do. But the hard part was never building the prototype. It was having the discipline to kill the ones that didn't earn investment, including ones I liked.</p>
    </section>

